From b3e2ac9db890dacd2a3ace61a46ba1270c219a10 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 08:54:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Material transfer to g4ants3 by index: one record per
 ants3 material

RunSet: the four name-keyed lists (Materials, MaterialsFromNist,
MaterialsFromNCrystal, MaterialsWithCustomMeanExcitationEnergy) are
replaced by one array of AG4MaterialRecord, indexed by the ants3
material index:
  {Name, GdmlName, Type: Direct|Nist|NCrystal, NistName, NCrystal,
   MeanExEnergy_eV}
Name is the original ants3 name, GdmlName is the GDML export name from
the previous commit. The g4ants3 GDML export now uses those names.

g4ants3: SessionManager::setupMaterials() replaces updateMaterials().
For every record it finds the GDML material by its exact GdmlName and
builds a dedicated Geant4 material:
  - Direct:   the GDML material, renamed to the original ants3 name,
              with the custom mean excitation energy if requested
  - Nist:     a new G4Material derived from the NIST base material, so
              several ants3 materials using the same NIST material no
              longer collapse into one G4Material. It keeps the NIST name:
              HP thermal scattering recognizes materials by NIST name
              (G4ParticleHPThermalScatteringNames, e.g. G4_WATER + H)
  - NCrystal: as before, renamed to the original ants3 name
Logical volumes are then switched to the final materials by pointer in
one pass over G4LogicalVolumeStore (replaces the recursive name match).
The result is kept in MaterialsByIndex (ants3 index -> G4Material*).
Two records resolving to the same G4Material object stop the session.

Material-limited sources: the source keeps the ants3 material name and
g4ants3 resolves it via findMaterialByAntsName() to the dedicated
material; replaceMatNameInMatLimitedSources() is removed.

Dropped: the check that a NIST-based material may not be named like its
NIST material; with derived materials this no longer causes ambiguity.

